Graham Scan Visualizer
Elki Laranas
Tufts CS163 Computational Geometry

Instructions: 

    Deployed site: https://graham-visual.vercel.app/
    Local: cd into root folder, run "npm run dev" in terminal

Intersting things I learned/did:

My biggest takeaway from this was that I couldn't just write a normal algorithm
loop for a visualizer. I had to turn the whole thing into a sort of "state machine" 
by saving snapshots at each step, of the stack, the candidate point, and the 
left/right turn boolean just so the "Next" and "Prev" buttons would work.

I also realized I could reuse code for the upper and lower hulls by reusing the
exact same buildHull logic for the upper hull just by reversing the sorted 
points array and running it again. I was initially worried about differentiating
between the logic for the two, but I found that it worked just by doing that.

I’ve used Plotly before for previous projects but I haven't applied it to anything
geometric yet. It was cool to see how well I could use it to draw shapes and display
a geometric algorithm from this class, although the algorithm I chose was pretty simple.

I expected the geometry part to be complicated, but computing "Left" or "Right" turns 
was simple and only required one cross-product formula, which made the logic straightforward
to implement in one helper function.